    "title": "English definitional verbs",
    "language": "en",
    "description": "Verbs (and a few fixed markers) that assert identity, a name/alias, a meaning, a category, or an equivalence between two expressions \u2014 the predicates that make a sentence a DEFINITION rather than an assertion about the world. Grouped by what the definition asserts: equative, naming, defining, classifying, meronymic (part-whole, adjacent), and equivalence. Each entry gives a common surface pattern.",
    "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
    "credits": "../CREDITS.md",
    "source_notes": "Directly serves definitional-antecedent coreference (the 'referred to as \"the Company\"' aliasing pattern in SEC filings and contracts) and definitional/glossary grounding. The equative/naming/defining distinction is standard semantics; the list is a compilation, not a novel taxonomy.",
    "status": "seed \u2014 assistant-drafted and unreviewed; the subtype assignments in particular need a human check (describe/represent/include sit on category boundaries).",
    "method": "assistant",
    "reviewed": false
  },
